From the "I can't wait for the beta test" department....

    For those of you into computer gaming and the new realms
    of excitment opening with each advance in technology, comes
    this item reported in the June 1994 copy of "Clinician
    Reviews".

    Reporting on scientific papers presented at the American
    Roentgen Ray Society's annual meeting, held in New Orleans
    this past April, the following report was presented...

      "A new CT scanning technique that is still in development
      could revolutionize colon cancer screening by reducing risk
      and examination time for patients.  The new method, according
      to David J. Vining, MD, Assistant Professor of Radiology at
      Bowman Gray School of Medicine in Winston-Salem, NC, uses
      a combination of helical CT and virtual reality to create
      'virtual colonoscopy' images...

      "With helical CT, patients prepare their colons (!) with a
      liquid diet and laxatives prior to the procedure.  Then a
      small catheter is inserted in the patient to inflate the
      colon.  The actual helical CT exam takes 30 to 40 seconds,
      after which the patient is free to leave.  The radiologic
      clinician then takes the images produced by the helical CT
      and uses a computer program to create a virtual reality
      image.  This allows the radiologist to view the entire
      colon from the inside and search for polyps and masses."

Bet you'll be dying to see the hint booklet for this one, eh
gamers?  Something like "check behind that little lump of stuff
just past the third haustra on the right after you pass the splenic
flexure".

Oh, Brave New World!



